{"author":{"id":"c58c423763989724eca8e3600c498f8f8bddd3e63f2ca8d0fc15fa72a53b3eda"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794108224,"nonce":"7HyhB+8nTh5vy43WDwJJR7VXRyM=","title":"French past participle agreement comes from a rule; use Wiktionary's participle entries instead","message":"Wiktionary's verb table gives only the masculine singular past participle, so lang/fr/lexicon.py generates the feminine and plurals by rule (+e/+s/+es, dû -\u003e due, dissous -\u003e dissoute) and leaves a hand-written list unchanged (été, pu, fallu, plu, ri, souri, nui, suffi, semblé). The list isn't complete: other avoir-only intransitive verbs get forms that don't occur (dormie).\n\nThe French extract has separate verb entries for participles with their own forms (masculine plural, feminine, feminine plural; about 6500 of each). Reading those would replace the rule. Needs a pass over entries before converting, since the converter sees one entry at a time.","files":null}]}